<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nicomedes I of Bithynia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 255 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nicomedes I was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bithynia">Bithynia</a> in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> from about 278 to 255 BCE. To fight his brother and the Seleucid king Antiochus I, he invited bands of Celtic warriors across the Bosporus as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> in 278 BCE; they later settled in central Anatolia as the Galatians. In 264 BCE he founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicomedia">Nicomedia</a> as his capital, which became one of the great cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nicomedes-i-of-bithynia.jpg" alt="Nicomedes I of Bithynia" width="320" height="153" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coin_of_Nikomedes_I_of_Bithynia.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
